<span class="eyebrow">Editor</span>
<h1>Assistant</h1>

<p class="doc-lead">
  A chat and agent panel on the right of the editor, opened with <kbd>Alt</kbd> + <kbd>A</kbd> or from the right rail.
</p>

<Callout variant="info" title="You need an Anthropic API key">
  Set it in <strong>Settings › Assistant</strong>. It is kept in a credential vault of its own and sent only to Anthropic,
  with each request. Every message is billed to your key; the conversation shows what it has cost.
</Callout>

<h2>What is on the panel</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Conversation</strong></td><td>The title is a switcher: your conversations, today's first, each with the model it used, when it was last touched and what it cost. The pencil renames the open one, the <strong>…</strong> menu deletes it, and <strong>New conversation</strong> starts another. The open one shows as saved.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Context</strong></td><td>How full the model's window is, as a bar split into <em>instructions &amp; tools</em>, <em>files &amp; search results</em> and the <em>conversation</em>. It stays calm until 75%, turns amber there and red past 90%, and offers <strong>Compact conversation…</strong>: it clears the output of older tool calls, then the model folds everything before your last message into a summary — and what you type in the dialog is what the summary must not lose, as with <code>/compact "…"</code> in Claude Code. A conversation that reaches 90% of the largest window you chose is compacted automatically (clearing first, summarising only if that is not enough), and the thread says so. Compacting costs one request, added to the conversation's cost. Open the line for the breakdown. Every figure is an estimate (about four characters to a token); the exact count comes back with each answer.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Thread</strong></td><td>Your messages on the right; the assistant's as flowing text and highlighted code, with the tools it used folded into one line that opens on demand ("Read 2 files · Searched once"), and the changes it proposes as a card listing the files with their added and removed lines. The assistant answers in Markdown, rendered as headings, lists, tables, quotes, highlighted code and bold text, and the text is selectable. Hover an answer to copy it or to <strong>open it as a document</strong> — a Markdown editor (live preview, as in Bennu's <code>.md</code> tabs) where you can edit the answer, copy it or save it to a file; the conversation itself is not changed. Hover one of your own messages to copy it. <strong>Stop</strong> (the square where Send was) ends a run; what it had written stays.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Review</strong></td><td>Opens the proposed changes as a diff, unified or side by side, with the files in a list on the left (<kbd>↑</kbd> / <kbd>↓</kbd> move between them). In manual mode the window is where you decide: <strong>Apply all</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Enter</kbd>) or <strong>Reject all</strong>. A rejected card turns red and keeps its files readable; an applied one stays as a record and can be undone.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Model</strong></td><td>Chosen at the composer, with what each is for, the size of its window and its price per million tokens (input / output). The list and the prices are a file, <code>models.toml</code>, built in; <strong>Settings › Assistant › Models and prices</strong> creates your own copy (<code>assistant-models.toml</code> in Bennu's config folder) where an entry with the same id replaces the built-in one and any other id is added — so a price change or a new model needs an edit and a <em>Reload</em>, not a new build. An entry gives <code>[model.price]</code> (<code>input</code>, <code>output</code>, and optionally <code>cache_read</code>, <code>cache_write_5m</code>, <code>cache_write_1h</code>) and, for a model that charges more for long prompts, <code>[model.long_prompt]</code> with <code>over_tokens</code> and its own rates. Cost is worked out request by request, with cache reads and writes at their own rates.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Context window</strong></td><td>The largest window the assistant may use in this conversation: 50K up to 1M. A size the model cannot hold is listed but cannot be picked, and changing to a smaller model shrinks the window to fit.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Questions</strong></td><td>Where it would otherwise guess, the assistant asks. Each question is a column of choices, one per row — pick one, or tick several — with <em>Other…</em> always there to type your own. When it asks several things at once, a strip of tabs names them (a tick once answered): jump to any, or use <strong>Back</strong> and <strong>Next</strong>, and the last step sends all the answers together, so changing an earlier one costs nothing. Answered, the card shrinks to what you decided and stays in the thread.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Reach</strong></td><td>The shield beside <em>Add context</em> sets what it may touch: <em>this file</em>, <em>this project</em> (the default), <em>all open projects</em>, or <em>anywhere on disk</em>, which never asks. Reaching for anything outside the chosen scope raises one prompt that settles both questions: <strong>Deny</strong>, <strong>Allow reading</strong> or <strong>Allow reading &amp; writing</strong> — for that folder, for the session. Secrets (<code>.env</code>, keys) and <code>.git</code> are never written. It may also be allowed to edit files and to use the Temp folders. Deleting is not a setting: every deletion asks.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Dry run</strong></td><td>Turn it on from the <strong>…</strong> menu and Send shows the request instead of making it: the endpoint, the headers (the key masked, never shown), the JSON body exactly as it would go, and where its size comes from — instructions, tool definitions, conversation — with a rough cost. <strong>Show what would be sent…</strong> opens the same window at any time.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Composer</strong></td><td>One quiet line under the text: the <strong>model</strong>, a <strong>settings</strong> menu and, in an agent conversation, the <strong>Auto</strong> switch. The settings menu holds the rest, each as a submenu showing its current value: <em>Effort</em>, <em>Context window</em>, <em>Reach</em> and <em>Permissions</em>. Chips above the text show what the message carries: the file you are in, the lines you selected, and the instruction files in play.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Agent · Chat</strong></td><td>The switch in the header chooses between two kinds of conversation, kept apart. <em>Agent</em> works in the project through Bennu's tools. <em>Chat</em> has no tools on the project: it cannot read, search or change anything, and sees only what you type or select (its one tool is the help about Bennu itself) — for a question that has nothing to do with the project, or when you want to be sure nothing is touched. Each has its own list of conversations and its own home screen, and a conversation is one or the other for good; opening a chat from the list switches the panel to Chat.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Home</strong></td><td>With no conversation open the panel shows its home: the mode you are in, the file and instruction files it would work with, a few things to begin with (an agent card sends a request at once; a chat card leaves the start of a sentence in the composer for you to finish) and your recent conversations. The panel opens on it.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Project instructions</strong></td><td>The project's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> and <code>AGENTS.md</code>, and your own <code>assistant-instructions.md</code> in Bennu's config folder, are read into the assistant's instructions, so it follows the rules a team has already written down (the build command, conventions, what not to touch). Each file is cut at 16 KB and all together at 40 KB, and a file whose text repeats another is sent once. They appear as chips above the text; the instructions never override the access prompts or the ban on a shell. Turn it off in <strong>Settings › Assistant</strong>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Git</strong></td><td>Off by default; <strong>Settings › Assistant › Git</strong> turns it on. The assistant can then read the repository — status, diff, log and blame — and stage, unstage and commit, through Corvus's own git tools; it cannot push. Reading means commit messages and authors go to the provider, so <em>Hide authors</em> (on by default) shows the model each person as a made-up id such as <code>Author-7f3a9c</code>: the same person is always the same id, and the ids are turned back into names in what you read and in anything the assistant sends to git, so a commit message that credits an id credits the real person. A commit always asks first, even in Auto. Git is not available in a chat.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Plan</strong></td><td>For work with several steps the assistant writes a plan first and ticks the steps off as it goes. The card in the thread lists them — done, in progress, still to do — with a bar and <em>3 of 7 · 43%</em>. The percentage is steps done over steps planned: it says how much of the list is done, not how long the rest will take, and it goes down if the plan grows. It costs a little: the assistant writes the list again each time it changes, a few hundred tokens of output per update — in the order of a few cents for a task of several steps, a few percent of what such a task costs — and it is told to send updates alongside the tools it is already calling, so they add no requests of their own. Questions and small changes get no plan. <strong>Step plan</strong> in the composer's settings menu (and in Settings › Assistant) switches it off: the tool and its instruction are then not sent at all.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Spending limits</strong></td><td>In <strong>Settings › Assistant › Spending limits</strong>, limits in euros: per conversation, per day (from midnight, your time), per week (from Monday) and per month. <code>0</code> switches one off; the defaults are €5 a conversation, €10 a day and €50 a month. A conversation or a period at its limit does not start another request, and a run in progress stops before the request that would take it over — the thread says which limit. The composer flags a limit from 80% (adjustable) and shows what the conversation has cost. Costs are recorded per run in a ledger of their own, so deleting a conversation does not hand its money back. Prices are in dollars, so amounts are converted at an exchange rate you keep by hand; with no usable rate a dollar counts as a euro.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Ask from the editor</strong></td><td>A <strong>✦</strong> in the margin sits on the line the caret is on, or where a selection starts, and offers what fits what is there: <em>Fix this error</em> or <em>Explain this problem</em> when the editor has found a problem on the line; <em>Explain</em>, <em>Look for bugs</em>, <em>Suggest improvements</em> and <em>Write tests</em> for a selection; <em>Explain</em>, <em>Write tests</em>, <em>Where is it used?</em> and <em>Write documentation</em> for a declaration; and always <em>Ask about this…</em>, which leaves the start of a message in the composer. The request carries the file, the lines and the code itself, and starts a conversation of its own. <em>Ask the assistant about this…</em> in the Command Palette does the same from the keyboard, opening the menu at the caret. In a Markdown file, selecting a passage shows a small button at its end: <em>Improve the writing</em>, <em>Make it shorter</em>, <em>Say more</em>, <em>Fix spelling and grammar</em>, <em>Translate</em>. In the Run and Build output, the line that starts a <strong>stack trace</strong> has a <em>Fix with the assistant</em> button that sends the trace and asks for the cause in the project.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Help with Bennu</strong></td><td>Ask how to do something in Bennu — a feature, a shortcut, where a panel is — and the assistant looks it up in Bennu's own documentation (and a map of the window's buttons and shortcuts) instead of answering from memory, which for a tool it has never seen would be invented. It works in a chat too. If the documentation has nothing, it says so.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Comments</strong></td><td>In a Java or Rust file, a <strong>✦</strong> in the gutter sits on every doc comment — and, under the caret, on a declaration that has none. It opens: <em>Improve this comment</em>, <em>Check against the code</em> (parameters, return value, exceptions, statements the code contradicts), <em>Make it shorter</em>, <em>Translate to English / Italian</em>, or <em>Write documentation</em>. The request carries the file, the lines and the declaration, so there is nothing to type; the result comes back as a proposal like any other edit.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Settings</strong></td><td>The <strong>…</strong> menu opens <strong>Settings › Assistant</strong>: the API key (kept in a credential vault of its own, apart from the one holding your git tokens, and never in a config file), the model, window and mode a new conversation starts with, and the same reach choices plus the extra folders it may work in.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Manual · Auto</strong></td><td><em>Manual</em>: you approve every edit — reject, review the diff, or apply. <em>Auto</em>: edits are applied as they are proposed, each one can be undone, and the card stays as a record.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>How it is meant to work</h2>
<ul>
  <li>It reads and edits the project only through Bennu's own tools — files, search, usages, beans, endpoints, diagnostics, and the refactoring engine (rename, add parameter, change case) — never a shell. <code>rename_symbol</code> and <code>add_parameter</code> work from the project index and change every file they touch as one change — proposed for you to review in Manual mode, applied in Auto — and a renamed public type's file is renamed with it.</li>
  <li><strong>Several changes at once.</strong> <code>edit_files</code> makes a list of exact edits across files as one change — all or nothing, one request — and <code>replace_in_files</code> replaces text or a regular expression across a folder (with a dry run first, and a limit on how many files it will touch). <code>structural_replace</code> rewrites code by its shape using Bennu's structural search, which is safer than a regular expression. All of them arrive as a proposal in Manual mode and are applied, and recorded in Local History, in Auto.</li>
  <li><strong>Local History.</strong> It can list a file's revisions, show what changed, list deleted files and restore one — a restore is an edit like any other, proposed or applied by your mode.</li>
  <li><strong>Showing you things.</strong> Ask to see the vulnerabilities, the problems, the module graph or Find in Files and it opens that window instead of describing it.</li>
  <li><strong>Tool families.</strong> Settings › Assistant › Tools it may use switches off groups of tools — reading and search, project intelligence, editing, refactoring, structural replace, Local History, build and run. A group that is off is not offered at all.</li>
  <li><strong>Several projects.</strong> With the reach set to <em>All open projects</em> the assistant works across the projects open in the workspace — the one you are in first, up to the number set in Settings › Assistant › Projects at once (6 by default). It reads, searches and changes in all of them: each is named in its instructions, a tool takes a project by name (or <code>name:path</code>), the text search and the file finder take <code>project: "all"</code>, and a change goes to the project that owns the file, which keeps its own Local History. A proposal names a file in another project as <code>name:path</code>.</li>
  <li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Enter</kbd> sends; <kbd>Enter</kbd> is a new line.</li>
  <li>The file you are in and the lines you have selected travel with the message, shown as chips above the text; the selection is read when you click into the composer.</li>
</ul>
